1
00:00:22,443 --> 00:00:28,340
It was here in rural South Vietnam that America and her allies met their darkest hour.

2
00:00:29,240 --> 00:00:34,340
This tropical land of rice paddies, small villages, and peasant farmers

3
00:00:34,341 --> 00:00:37,900
soon turned into a living hell for the G.I.s

4
00:00:37,901 --> 00:00:42,440
as they tried to wage war against a dedicated and determined enemy.

5
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:50,180
To combat America's superior firepower and complete control of the air,

6
00:00:50,620 --> 00:00:52,900
the Viet Cong adopted a brilliant strategy.

7
00:00:53,720 --> 00:00:55,020
At the heart of their resistance,

8
00:00:55,021 --> 00:00:59,800
a deadly spider's web of fiercely defended tunnels and subterranean bunkers

9
00:00:59,801 --> 00:01:02,620
under the rice fields and jungles of South Vietnam.

10
00:01:03,940 --> 00:01:08,160
The communist fighters literally lived, ate, and slept under the battlefield.

11
00:01:09,420 --> 00:01:12,340
They had hospitals, classrooms, and armament workshops.

12
00:01:13,080 --> 00:01:17,100
From these fortified nests, the guerrillas sprung their deadly ambushes.

13
00:01:19,100 --> 00:01:24,020
But a courageous few American and Australian foot soldiers, the Tunnel Rats,

14
00:01:24,021 --> 00:01:26,220
dared to staunch the enemy tide.

15
00:01:26,700 --> 00:01:28,500
It was their terrifying task

16
00:01:28,501 --> 00:01:32,140
to chase the enemy down into their booby-trapped retreat.

17
00:01:34,180 --> 00:01:37,840
You can get down in a tunnel and start closing in on you

18
00:01:37,841 --> 00:01:39,580
where you hear noises and you start shooting

19
00:01:39,581 --> 00:01:42,960
and you can get cranked up real easy and lose it completely

20
00:01:42,961 --> 00:01:48,560
and probably make you go insane if you're not careful.

21
00:01:49,740 --> 00:01:52,740
When you expanded your lungs, when you took a breath,

22
00:01:53,460 --> 00:01:54,420
You're kind of wedded in.

23
00:01:55,160 --> 00:01:58,400
Now, if you can picture yourself being stuck, if there was a cave in or something,

24
00:01:58,840 --> 00:02:00,280
I can't imagine a worse way to die.

25
00:02:01,480 --> 00:02:07,560
The tunnel rats would soon distinguish themselves for their uncommon valor and selfless commitment to duty.

26
00:02:09,240 --> 00:02:10,800
You took pride in what you did.

27
00:02:10,900 --> 00:02:12,140
There wasn't very many of us.

28
00:02:12,600 --> 00:02:15,720
But if you did it well, you know you did something worthwhile.

29
00:02:16,500 --> 00:02:18,880
Because whatever you unearthed, whatever you uncovered,

30
00:02:18,881 --> 00:02:23,700
whether it be intelligence, maps, we get the edge on the enemy.

31
00:02:24,860 --> 00:02:29,360
The most dangerous part would be psyching up to get into the tunnel.

32
00:02:29,700 --> 00:02:33,100
That was the part that was really the most frightening

33
00:02:33,101 --> 00:02:37,300
because you didn't know what you were getting into.

34
00:02:38,860 --> 00:02:43,960
When the U.S. and Allied troops first arrived in Vietnam in the early 1960s,

35
00:02:43,961 --> 00:02:47,780
they had no idea that the enemy were under their very feet.

36
00:02:47,781 --> 00:02:51,720
On the surface, the tunnel entrances were carefully camouflaged.

37
00:02:52,580 --> 00:02:55,780
VC guerrillas appeared and disappeared down them at will.

38
00:02:57,140 --> 00:02:58,280
They were so well concealed.

39
00:02:58,920 --> 00:03:02,080
I mean, we probably walked over thousands of them, never knew it.

40
00:03:02,800 --> 00:03:04,400
Especially in the Coo Chi area.

41
00:03:05,100 --> 00:03:09,000
That's where the majority of all the tunnels were.

42
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:14,140
The small town of Coo Chi, just 50 miles north of Saigon,

43
00:03:14,300 --> 00:03:16,880
was at the heart of the Viet Cong secret network.

44
00:03:16,881 --> 00:03:23,580
By 1965, the system snaked nearly 200 miles under villages, huts and fields.

45
00:03:23,920 --> 00:03:28,680
It weaved from the Ho Chi Minh Trail in the north to the gates of Saigon in the south.

46
00:03:32,800 --> 00:03:38,600
Under the battlefield was a system of tunnels connecting communities from village to village.

47
00:03:40,400 --> 00:03:43,800
This was not the first time tunnels have been used in war.

48
00:03:44,520 --> 00:03:48,720
In fact, tunnels and underground fortifications had been used for thousands of years.

49
00:03:49,540 --> 00:03:54,540
Among the first troops with a specific mission to search and destroy the enemy's underground bunkers

50
00:03:54,541 --> 00:03:59,035
were British sappers in World War I, and most famously, the U.S.

51
00:03:59,036 --> 00:04:00,720
Marines in World War II.

52
00:04:01,440 --> 00:04:09,640
As early as 1943, the Japanese dug confounding subterranean defenses on the coral atolls of the Central Pacific,

53
00:04:09,641 --> 00:04:16,800
penetrating the caves and hidden bunkers on the many islands like saipan alelu iwo jima and okinawa

54
00:04:16,801 --> 00:04:24,140
became a challenging and dangerous task for these first u.s troops to be called tunnel rats we

55
00:04:24,141 --> 00:04:31,060
developed this took us some time and a little bit of blood we developed techniques to deal with

56
00:04:31,061 --> 00:04:38,780
these things uh above all this meant ceiling entrances ceiling exits going in with flamethrowers

57
00:04:38,781 --> 00:04:41,580
frankly entombing or killing the people inside.

58
00:04:41,960 --> 00:04:43,340
And you're able to do this.

59
00:04:44,020 --> 00:04:45,920
In 1946 in Vietnam,

60
00:04:46,440 --> 00:04:49,140
the Viet Minh, as the resistance fighters were then called,

61
00:04:49,480 --> 00:04:51,980
began digging tunnels and underground bunkers

62
00:04:51,981 --> 00:04:52,980
to fight the French,

63
00:04:53,300 --> 00:04:55,540
who returned to Vietnam after World War II

64
00:04:55,541 --> 00:04:59,580
to reoccupy the former colony they called Indochina.

65
00:05:01,160 --> 00:05:03,320
In the spring of 1954,

66
00:05:03,700 --> 00:05:05,620
the Viet Minh defeated the French

67
00:05:05,621 --> 00:05:08,360
in the celebrated battle for Diem Bien Phu.

68
00:05:08,780 --> 00:05:12,780
It spelt the end for the French and their empirical aspirations.

69
00:05:12,781 --> 00:05:21,780
For the next ten years, while the Army of South Vietnam, or ARVN, and the Saigon government were mired in corruption and coups,

70
00:05:21,781 --> 00:05:27,780
the communist guerrillas now called Viet Cong, or VC, took control of rural South Vietnam.

71
00:05:29,780 --> 00:05:32,422
When they arrived in 1965, the U.S.

72
00:05:32,423 --> 00:05:37,780
armies, including the 1st and 25th Divisions, stepped into a bear trap.

73
00:05:37,781 --> 00:05:41,180
The enemy, battle-hardened communist guerrillas.

74
00:05:46,420 --> 00:05:49,880
The Americans thought they could win the war easily.

75
00:05:51,160 --> 00:05:54,500
But in reality, it wasn't that simple.

76
00:06:02,540 --> 00:06:06,080
When we lost in combat, we do not take it as a loss.

77
00:06:06,081 --> 00:06:11,160
It gave us other presence and strength, which we would take into the next battle.

78
00:06:14,140 --> 00:06:17,060
From the hidden underground bunkers and spider holes,

79
00:06:17,460 --> 00:06:21,720
the Viet Cong guerrillas sprung devastating ambushes and stealthy night attacks

80
00:06:21,721 --> 00:06:25,220
that inflicted horrific casualties on American forces.

81
00:06:26,620 --> 00:06:30,640
You'd be walking through an area, and you're really thinking there's going to be a bunker over here in this hedgerow,

82
00:06:30,720 --> 00:06:32,520
and there might be a sniper up in this tree and that kind of thing.

83
00:06:32,521 --> 00:06:37,440
but most of the danger came from walking to an area that was in festivities, tunnels,

84
00:06:37,880 --> 00:06:41,020
and what we call spider holes, just small, you know, maybe just big around,

85
00:06:41,860 --> 00:06:45,880
holes that these guys could pop up out of, take a shot, hit a guy in the back, pop back down.

86
00:06:46,740 --> 00:06:52,360
The young American and Allied soldiers on patrol in the thick jungle canopy of South Vietnam

87
00:06:52,361 --> 00:06:59,700
were left chasing shadows in an alien environment where violence and fear characterized their daily existence.

88
00:07:00,660 --> 00:07:03,040
He dreaded being in the jungle where you can't see.

89
00:07:04,040 --> 00:07:04,820
It was scary.

90
00:07:05,580 --> 00:07:09,220
There were times where some jungle parts were even at 12 o'clock noon,

91
00:07:09,340 --> 00:07:12,260
you'd have to use a flashlight to see because it was that dark.

92
00:07:13,260 --> 00:07:15,740
Everybody was the same. Everybody depended on each other.

93
00:07:15,920 --> 00:07:19,440
You know, sure, we get scared as hell sometimes,

94
00:07:19,560 --> 00:07:22,140
and sometimes you get so damn scared you couldn't move.

95
00:07:23,080 --> 00:07:28,340
To survive the horror of combat, soldiers quickly learned to suppress their emotions.

96
00:07:29,700 --> 00:07:35,620
On Operation Crimp, I think the first day there was four boats killed,

97
00:07:36,580 --> 00:07:41,040
and I walked past their bodies and felt nothing, absolutely nothing.

98
00:07:42,280 --> 00:07:47,160
The Viet Cong became brilliantly adept at making savage booby traps.

99
00:07:47,760 --> 00:07:49,560
Some devices were brutally simple.

100
00:07:50,020 --> 00:07:52,340
When a trooper stepped onto one of these man traps,

101
00:07:52,760 --> 00:07:55,620
the wooden spikes impaled and incapacitated him.

102
00:07:56,600 --> 00:08:03,160
The guerrillas became especially skilled at turning unexploded American ordnance into devastating booby traps.

103
00:08:04,060 --> 00:08:09,340
Most of the time we found booby traps, it was because somebody triggered it.

104
00:08:10,220 --> 00:08:19,220
Infantryman and soon-to-be tunnel rat Gary Schooler remembers vividly his first patrol in the Hobo Woods, just east of the Iron Triangle.

105
00:08:19,960 --> 00:08:23,580
It was the first time I saw anybody killed on my first day out.

106
00:08:24,420 --> 00:08:25,460
And some guy sat down.

107
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:27,000
We call them stick mines.

108
00:08:27,160 --> 00:08:29,040
It's a vertical bamboo stick.

109
00:08:29,160 --> 00:08:29,860
You can tilt it.

110
00:08:29,861 --> 00:08:30,700
You don't even have to step on it.

111
00:08:30,720 --> 00:08:32,100
You can just tilt it and it goes off.

112
00:08:32,320 --> 00:08:33,700
And then there's horrible screaming.

113
00:08:33,900 --> 00:08:35,060
It's a real high shriek.

114
00:08:35,300 --> 00:08:36,860
It's just ear piercing.

115
00:08:37,180 --> 00:08:38,060
So you don't forget it.

116
00:08:38,080 --> 00:08:39,160
When you hear that sound, you don't forget it.

117
00:08:39,180 --> 00:08:40,140
You know the movie travel.

118
00:08:42,500 --> 00:08:47,860
In October 1965, 68 Australian engineers, or sappers,

119
00:08:47,861 --> 00:08:50,860
from three field troops arrived in Vietnam

120
00:08:50,861 --> 00:08:54,220
primarily to help locate and disarm the booby traps.

121
00:08:55,320 --> 00:08:59,720
However, they soon made a critical discovery that changed the course of the war.

122
00:09:00,440 --> 00:09:04,400
25-year-old Captain Alex Sandy McGregor led the way.

123
00:09:06,140 --> 00:09:11,120
We came across this tunnel within a village, which was an enemy village.

124
00:09:11,760 --> 00:09:14,380
What happened was that I went down the tunnel,

125
00:09:14,381 --> 00:09:19,220
and I can remember my sergeant, my staff sergeant actually,

126
00:09:19,380 --> 00:09:25,600
holding my legs with a rope because I went down head first.

127
00:09:26,720 --> 00:09:31,020
This first tunnel turned out to be a relatively short escape tunnel from a village hut.

128
00:09:31,380 --> 00:09:34,900
It would, however, be dwarfed by an incredible discovery on their next mission.

129
00:09:35,440 --> 00:09:39,400
The Australians were about to find the enemy's massive secret tunnel labyrinth,

130
00:09:39,401 --> 00:09:42,140
but their success would come at a deadly price.

131
00:09:43,140 --> 00:09:49,300
Many innovative weapons, including the Claymore Mines, were introduced to combat during the Vietnam War.

132
00:09:49,840 --> 00:09:54,660
Among them were the F-4s, the AH-1G Huey Cobra, and the gunship.

133
00:09:55,040 --> 00:09:57,440
The Tunnels of Vietnam will be right back.

134
00:10:00,880 --> 00:10:08,600
On January 7, 1966, American and Allied forces, under the direction of General William Westmoreland,

135
00:10:08,601 --> 00:10:14,720
launched a massive attack, spearheaded by the pride of America's military, the 1st Infantry Division.

136
00:10:16,480 --> 00:10:20,720
The strike was aimed at the heart of the Viet Cong war machine in South Vietnam.

137
00:10:21,760 --> 00:10:25,880
They moved into the Hobo Woods, just east of the Iron Triangle.

138
00:10:29,160 --> 00:10:34,440
Helicopters, tanks, armored personnel carriers, and more than 8,000 men joined the battle.

139
00:10:34,441 --> 00:10:44,260
In one huge blow, America's leaders planned to find and destroy the Viet Cong Politico headquarters and effectively end the war.

140
00:10:48,040 --> 00:10:55,080
The Australian engineers of three field troop were assigned to support the crack 173rd Airborne Brigade.

141
00:10:55,660 --> 00:10:58,960
They deployed from their base at Bien Hoa, just east of Saigon.

142
00:11:00,360 --> 00:11:03,540
The Australians headed for the south edge of the Hobo Woods.

143
00:11:03,541 --> 00:11:07,540
If all went according to plan, the enemy would be swept down towards them.

144
00:11:08,660 --> 00:11:10,420
Then the Australians would cut them off.

145
00:11:10,880 --> 00:11:12,160
But the plan backfired.

146
00:11:12,680 --> 00:11:15,160
The enemy was already waiting when they landed.

147
00:11:16,340 --> 00:11:21,040
As soon as we landed, I knew there was something up.

148
00:11:21,680 --> 00:11:23,440
There were shots coming out.

149
00:11:23,600 --> 00:11:25,580
There were guns going off.

150
00:11:25,680 --> 00:11:27,500
We were being mortared at that time.

151
00:11:27,501 --> 00:11:36,600
The thing that no training will ever prepare you for is the sound.

152
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:38,140
The sound and the smell.

153
00:11:40,140 --> 00:11:42,540
The noise is indescribable.

154
00:11:43,520 --> 00:11:46,740
And of course the other thing is that time really elongates itself

155
00:11:46,741 --> 00:11:48,980
and things become very, very vivid.

156
00:11:49,740 --> 00:11:50,780
Australia are in action.

157
00:11:50,781 --> 00:12:01,300
In the midst of the chaos, an Australian infantryman spotted Viet Cong snipers firing from slits in what looked like an anthill.

158
00:12:02,200 --> 00:12:05,140
He crawled towards it and slung a grenade into the mound.

159
00:12:05,940 --> 00:12:08,660
He then called the engineers of three field troops.

160
00:12:10,740 --> 00:12:12,860
The Aussie sappers were ready.

161
00:12:13,240 --> 00:12:16,260
They had already devised a technique to deal with the tunnels.

162
00:12:16,261 --> 00:12:25,040
They first blew the top off to reveal an entrance, and then they used an industrial air blower called a Mighty Might and blew smoke down the hole.

163
00:12:26,240 --> 00:12:29,660
Then what we did was we blew down tear gas.

164
00:12:30,220 --> 00:12:36,740
The smoke was to find entrances, the tear gas was to clear the tunnel of enemy as far as we could.

165
00:12:36,741 --> 00:12:46,040
And then our boys wearing gas masks, torch in one hand, bayonet in the other, going down the tunnel.

166
00:12:47,040 --> 00:12:48,380
The tunnel was empty.

167
00:12:49,220 --> 00:12:51,600
They then prepared explosives to destroy it.

168
00:12:52,960 --> 00:12:56,320
They skillfully placed the charges to make the maximum impact.

169
00:12:57,600 --> 00:12:59,680
The charges that were used were all big.

170
00:13:01,420 --> 00:13:03,040
Military demolitions is like that.

171
00:13:03,140 --> 00:13:05,260
You make sure that the job is done properly the first time.

172
00:13:05,840 --> 00:13:06,940
So the charges were big.

173
00:13:07,880 --> 00:13:10,720
Less than a mile away, another tunnel had been found.

174
00:13:11,420 --> 00:13:14,980
Aussie Sapper Ray Forrester and his partner were sent to investigate.

175
00:13:14,981 --> 00:13:18,380
I was scared the whole time. I won't even deny it.

176
00:13:18,381 --> 00:13:23,360
And inside it was an eerie silence. You could kind of hear yourself breathing.

177
00:13:25,500 --> 00:13:28,760
Yeah, it was just very eerie.

178
00:13:30,120 --> 00:13:34,420
It was all slime on the walls. Whatever you touch was slimy.

179
00:13:37,160 --> 00:13:42,500
And I just felt like pigs to the slaughter because you're just rolling around in the mud.

180
00:13:42,501 --> 00:13:46,760
While simple tunnels and underground bunkers have been found before,

181
00:13:47,240 --> 00:13:52,520
the ones discovered during Operation Crimp were on a massive scale never seen before.

182
00:13:53,180 --> 00:13:56,160
They found whole guerrilla encampments underground,

183
00:13:56,820 --> 00:13:59,060
bunkers connected by tiny passageways.

184
00:13:59,740 --> 00:14:02,080
There were chambers that stored arms and food.

185
00:14:02,980 --> 00:14:05,780
They had sleeping quarters and areas to treat the wounded.

186
00:14:05,781 --> 00:14:13,020
The tunnel rats found hidden trap doors laced with booby traps that led to even deeper levels and more chambers.

187
00:14:13,960 --> 00:14:18,000
They had uncovered the Viet Cong's secret underground fortifications.

188
00:14:20,940 --> 00:14:29,020
When General Westmoreland was informed of the find, he ordered all tunnels be searched for men, intelligence, arms and supplies.

189
00:14:29,600 --> 00:14:31,240
Then they were to be destroyed.

190
00:14:32,240 --> 00:14:35,920
The tunnel rats, driven by duty and courage, went to work.

191
00:14:37,140 --> 00:14:42,720
When they found trap doors, they took even greater care in case it was armed with a booby trap.

192
00:14:43,400 --> 00:14:46,640
You'd use your fingers, you virtually went down and kissed it.

193
00:14:46,641 --> 00:14:51,360
That's how close you got, you know, just to make sure that you didn't miss anything.

194
00:14:51,700 --> 00:14:56,760
You can't miss anything, so you always just checked and double-checked.

195
00:14:56,761 --> 00:15:07,401
On day two of Operation Crimp, on January 8th, 1966, three field troops returned to the Hobo woods and parachuted into a hornet's nest.

196
00:15:07,402 --> 00:15:10,474
Incredibly, they landed on a VC tunnel.

197
00:15:10,475 --> 00:15:15,440
What they unearthed in it almost changed the course of the war.

198
00:15:16,220 --> 00:15:19,320
What we landed on was the headquarters of the Cholonghaden area.

199
00:15:19,500 --> 00:15:22,100
We actually even pulled out the hit list, right,

200
00:15:22,280 --> 00:15:25,140
that the Viet Cong had of the senior officers in Saigon

201
00:15:25,141 --> 00:15:27,380
in the order that they were going to be hit.

202
00:15:27,860 --> 00:15:30,460
The VC hit list whetted their curiosity.

203
00:15:31,280 --> 00:15:33,600
The tunnel rats had unearthed a gold mine.

204
00:15:33,920 --> 00:15:36,360
They descended deeper, down three levels,

205
00:15:36,620 --> 00:15:38,780
but stopped before opening a trap door.

206
00:15:39,820 --> 00:15:41,700
Had they gone through that trap door,

207
00:15:42,220 --> 00:15:44,060
we would have unleashed hell

208
00:15:44,061 --> 00:15:52,680
because that was the only entrance into the heavy headquarters for the Saigon area.

209
00:15:53,880 --> 00:15:56,620
One trap door and we could have changed the whole war.

210
00:15:57,700 --> 00:16:01,420
Really, it's terrifying to know that there were that many people down below us.

211
00:16:02,340 --> 00:16:07,620
But also, I don't know what would have happened, to be quite honest with you.

212
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:13,900
They only realised how close they were to the enemy headquarters after the war was over.

213
00:16:14,060 --> 00:16:20,940
Tragically, Operation Crimp also marked the first tunnel rat casualty.

214
00:16:21,680 --> 00:16:25,700
Corporal Bob Bartell got trapped in a small tunnel filled with smoke.

215
00:16:26,880 --> 00:16:31,140
Ray Forster went down the tunnel to try and pull his friend out of the tunnel shaft.

216
00:16:31,880 --> 00:16:35,820
I must have gone in a few times and I just couldn't move him.

217
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:39,020
Couldn't budge him. He was just stuck at the bottom of the strep door.

218
00:16:39,460 --> 00:16:42,400
I remember trying to dig out the tunnel entrance.

219
00:16:42,401 --> 00:16:44,100
but again that was

220
00:16:44,101 --> 00:16:46,360
that really wasn't satisfactory

221
00:16:46,361 --> 00:16:48,460
yeah

222
00:16:48,461 --> 00:16:50,540
I don't know at that stage

223
00:16:50,541 --> 00:16:53,000
I guess later on he was dead

224
00:16:53,001 --> 00:16:54,300
but I didn't know that at the time

225
00:16:54,301 --> 00:16:56,320
I thought I could try and get him out

226
00:16:56,321 --> 00:16:58,240
when I finally

227
00:16:58,241 --> 00:16:59,880
had to give in

228
00:16:59,881 --> 00:17:02,200
I went back up top

229
00:17:02,201 --> 00:17:04,020
and

230
00:17:04,021 --> 00:17:12,080
Ray Forrester

231
00:17:12,081 --> 00:17:17,360
was unable to save his friend and reluctantly following orders returned to the surface

232
00:17:17,361 --> 00:17:25,880
they realized that the smoke from the mighty might had sucked all the oxygen out of the tunnel

233
00:17:25,881 --> 00:17:33,560
and corporal bartell suffocated to death operation crimp was ended five days later

234
00:17:33,561 --> 00:17:36,520
and american and australian forces retreated

235
00:17:36,521 --> 00:17:42,620
With the incredible discovery of a vast enemy underground network, however,

236
00:17:43,180 --> 00:17:47,960
more tunnel rats would be needed to risk their lives and volunteer for this deadly mission.

237
00:17:51,540 --> 00:17:57,780
Westmoreland requested an additional 206,000 troops at the height of the Tet Offensive in 1968.

238
00:17:58,460 --> 00:18:02,280
This top-secret request was codenamed Operation Complete Victory.

239
00:18:02,800 --> 00:18:05,000
Modern Marvels will continue in a moment.

240
00:18:06,520 --> 00:18:14,220
January 1966, the 25th Tropic Lightning Infantry Division began to arrive in Vietnam.

241
00:18:15,180 --> 00:18:19,780
By the end of 1967, they were number 17,000 strong.

242
00:18:20,640 --> 00:18:27,460
Their mission was to eliminate the Viet Cong, destroy their subterranean stronghold, and protect Saigon.

243
00:18:28,380 --> 00:18:35,660
The 25th set up three camps, each bordering the treacherous Iron Triangle, Boiloi, and Hobo Woods.

244
00:18:36,520 --> 00:18:40,900
The main base at Ku Chi gave them quick access to these danger zones.

245
00:18:42,420 --> 00:18:46,120
Strategic planners, however, soon realized that they had made a fatal error.

246
00:18:46,600 --> 00:18:50,700
The 25th began taking fire inside the Ku Chi base camp.

247
00:18:53,460 --> 00:18:57,220
We had three tunnels under the 25th Division base camp.

248
00:18:57,520 --> 00:18:59,280
They were built before they arrived.

249
00:18:59,620 --> 00:19:01,860
When we attacked, they were taken by surprise.

250
00:19:01,861 --> 00:19:10,960
The 25th Infantry used earth movers called Rome Plows and explosives to destroy the tunnels under the base.

251
00:19:12,220 --> 00:19:18,360
It took them months, but eventually they were satisfied that they had eliminated the threat from within.

252
00:19:19,900 --> 00:19:24,760
Military planners were now just beginning to realize the extent of the tunnel problem.

253
00:19:25,980 --> 00:19:30,920
Despite the best efforts of the tunnel rats, who destroyed dozens of enemy bunkers and tunnels,

254
00:19:30,921 --> 00:19:33,500
the Viet Cong expanded their system.

255
00:19:34,840 --> 00:19:39,580
By 1967, the tunnel network reached into Saigon itself.

256
00:19:40,860 --> 00:19:44,680
The apex of the complex was literally in the Saigon suburbs.

257
00:19:44,960 --> 00:19:50,040
It would have been like if the Germans would have had a tunnel going into London in 1940.

258
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:54,600
Incredibly, even the commander of all Viet Cong forces,

259
00:19:55,160 --> 00:19:58,980
the leader of the resistance fighters in South Vietnam, Tran Bac Dang,

260
00:19:58,981 --> 00:20:01,520
would direct guerrilla operations by night

261
00:20:01,521 --> 00:20:05,480
and slip into Saigon and operate undetected during the day.

262
00:20:08,840 --> 00:20:12,500
I lived half my life in Saigon and half my life in the tunnels.

263
00:20:14,500 --> 00:20:17,820
My house was next door to the American ambassadors.

264
00:20:20,100 --> 00:20:22,780
On January 10, 1967,

265
00:20:22,781 --> 00:20:27,080
the 25th Infantry Division launched Operation Cedar Falls.

266
00:20:27,820 --> 00:20:33,640
The goal was to trap and crush the Viet Cong dug in and under the Iron Triangle.

267
00:20:34,700 --> 00:20:36,810
Newly recruited 25th Trooper C.W.

268
00:20:36,811 --> 00:20:41,540
Bowman arrived in Vietnam a few days before he went into the killing zone.

269
00:20:42,540 --> 00:20:46,820
When you were over there, you just had to reside to the fact that you were going to die.

270
00:20:47,700 --> 00:20:52,440
You know, you probably weren't going to make it, so you were going to do the best you could and fight as hard as you could.

271
00:20:52,441 --> 00:20:58,200
Patrol comrade Gary Heater was never too far from his close friend Bowman.

272
00:20:59,840 --> 00:21:03,720
You know, I kind of want to get attached to somebody so I can have a friend over there.

273
00:21:03,800 --> 00:21:09,520
And I didn't want to get too friendly to too many people in case, you know, you get hurt or lose somebody.

274
00:21:10,640 --> 00:21:12,660
But he's the only one I ever got close to.

275
00:21:14,320 --> 00:21:20,820
Incoming troops were filled with tales of the horrors faced by a small band of men, the Tunnel Rats,

276
00:21:20,821 --> 00:21:25,140
who dared to chase the fierce Viet Cong down into their tunnels.

277
00:21:26,460 --> 00:21:29,080
Sometimes they would put right underneath the entrance,

278
00:21:29,220 --> 00:21:32,660
they'd put a man trap, a large punji pit.

279
00:21:33,260 --> 00:21:37,400
Instead of landing on solid ground, you'd land into a punji pit.

280
00:21:39,540 --> 00:21:43,900
Operation Cedar Falls marked the initiation of a new breed of tunnel rat.

281
00:21:44,900 --> 00:21:46,700
So many tunnels were being uncovered

282
00:21:46,701 --> 00:21:49,460
and no time to wait for engineers to search them.

283
00:21:50,140 --> 00:21:55,220
The petrifying task was now open to regular foot soldiers gutsy enough to volunteer.

284
00:21:56,480 --> 00:21:59,680
In our unit, you know, it was, hey, you know, we need somebody to go in the tunnel.

285
00:21:59,880 --> 00:22:06,860
And we went, you know, and took a .45 and chambered around and cocked the hammer back and went in.

286
00:22:08,520 --> 00:22:12,020
Bowman and Heater volunteered for tunnel rat duty together.

287
00:22:13,000 --> 00:22:15,380
C.W. and I, we're closer than brothers.

288
00:22:16,360 --> 00:22:21,260
And I'd go in a lot of tunnels, and he'd come in behind me, or he'd go front.

289
00:22:24,400 --> 00:22:26,300
I felt safe when he was with me.

290
00:22:26,960 --> 00:22:28,700
That's the big thing.

291
00:22:30,080 --> 00:22:33,460
Gary and I were, you know, we were just a couple old G.I. Joes, you know,

292
00:22:33,461 --> 00:22:41,640
and looked at us, I guess, a challenger or whatever, and stepped up to do it.

293
00:22:41,641 --> 00:22:49,460
During Operation Cedar Falls, Bowman and Heater were initiated in the terrifying hazards of tunnel rat duty.

294
00:22:50,100 --> 00:22:58,929
He pushed the trap door open and nothing happened, you know, so Gary kind of stuck his arm up there with the light first and then just kind of eased up, looked over

295
00:22:58,930 --> 00:23:00,760
the edge and he dropped back down.

296
00:23:00,840 --> 00:23:04,600
He said, God, he says, there's a buoy trap up there, you know, and I said, what the hell is up there?

297
00:23:04,660 --> 00:23:11,380
He says, I don't know, he says, but it doesn't look good, you know, and we looked back up there and Claymore was.

298
00:23:11,381 --> 00:23:14,040
sitting up there on a tripod.

299
00:23:15,180 --> 00:23:18,180
Luckily, the Claymore mine was not booby-trapped,

300
00:23:18,580 --> 00:23:20,460
and Bowman and Heater surfaced safely.

301
00:23:22,720 --> 00:23:26,060
Operation Cedar Falls ended in frustration and failure.

302
00:23:26,720 --> 00:23:29,780
Although some documents, weapons, and ammunition were recovered,

303
00:23:30,280 --> 00:23:32,240
the enemy was far from routed.

304
00:23:33,620 --> 00:23:38,400
The guerrillas quickly and secretively rebuilt the tunnels that were destroyed.

305
00:23:38,401 --> 00:23:45,400
25th Infantry Tunnel Rats Bowman and Heater would soon return to action on new operations.

306
00:23:45,401 --> 00:23:49,400
Their biggest fear was meeting the enemy face to face.

307
00:23:49,401 --> 00:23:56,400
In the claustrophobic confines of the tunnel, the rats knew only one would emerge alive.

308
00:23:56,401 --> 00:24:03,400
Tunnel Rat Gary Heater was shocked by his first underground encounter.

309
00:24:03,401 --> 00:24:09,320
it would turn out to be a three four year old kid this kid was just sitting i guess his dad just

310
00:24:09,321 --> 00:24:16,380
left him there maybe he heard me coming but i just looked at him and he looked at me and you

311
00:24:16,381 --> 00:24:23,540
know i had a flashlight on him and i just kept on going it scared me he was just a kid but it could

312
00:24:23,541 --> 00:24:31,800
have been daddy the one time bowman went in a tunnel by himself he met the real enemy he crawled

313
00:24:31,801 --> 00:24:37,860
through a long, narrow passageway that opened up to a large chamber, a recovery room, with

314
00:24:37,861 --> 00:24:40,140
VC guerrillas lying in bunks.

315
00:24:41,080 --> 00:24:45,420
They had, like, their weapons up against the wall and stuff, and I kind of stumbled in

316
00:24:45,421 --> 00:24:46,121
there.

317
00:24:46,420 --> 00:24:52,280
When I came up in there and shined a light, you know, and boom, it was like Keystone cops.

318
00:24:52,500 --> 00:24:56,040
They started reaching for their weapons, and I started shooting.

319
00:24:56,540 --> 00:24:57,800
Flash, this happens.

320
00:24:57,940 --> 00:24:58,880
Flash, that happens.

321
00:25:00,060 --> 00:25:01,300
People hollering and screaming.

322
00:25:01,301 --> 00:25:03,580
people shooting, your heart's pounding.

323
00:25:03,780 --> 00:25:05,920
I mean, it's all reaction.

324
00:25:06,100 --> 00:25:07,220
You really don't think.

325
00:25:07,280 --> 00:25:08,160
You just start shooting.

326
00:25:08,480 --> 00:25:10,780
If you hesitate and think about it,

327
00:25:10,820 --> 00:25:12,400
you're the one that's probably going to die.

328
00:25:13,140 --> 00:25:17,400
I shot, you know, and get the hell out of there as fast as I could.

329
00:25:18,900 --> 00:25:23,520
Bowman remarkably survived this terrifying face-to-face unscathed.

330
00:25:25,540 --> 00:25:29,280
Yet his tunnel rat buddy Gary Heater wouldn't be so lucky.

331
00:25:29,900 --> 00:25:35,260
On his last mission before flying home, he stepped on a landmine known as a Bouncing Betty.

332
00:25:36,260 --> 00:25:39,700
I knew it was a Bouncing Betty because I heard the spring.

333
00:25:39,880 --> 00:25:42,280
As soon as I stepped, we was running through this minefield.

334
00:25:43,340 --> 00:25:44,040
And it went off.

335
00:25:47,440 --> 00:25:49,020
It didn't knock me unconscious.

336
00:25:49,300 --> 00:25:51,880
I was awake until I got to the hospital.

337
00:25:53,000 --> 00:25:55,560
But I couldn't see my leg.

338
00:25:55,561 --> 00:26:03,920
Gary Heater fortunately survived, but spent two years in a veterans hospital before he could walk again.

339
00:26:05,400 --> 00:26:10,420
As 1967 closed, American military planners raised the stakes.

340
00:26:11,100 --> 00:26:15,160
B-52 arc light strikes increased in ferocity and breadth.

341
00:26:15,161 --> 00:26:20,480
U.S. planes dropped massive payloads of iron bombs and chemical defoliants

342
00:26:20,481 --> 00:26:26,000
that would eventually reduce the Iron Triangle and the Hobo Woods to a desolate moonscape.

343
00:26:27,580 --> 00:26:32,360
Into this milestone, a new crop of America's youth arrived to do battle.

344
00:26:33,140 --> 00:26:35,280
The bravest volunteered to be tunnel rats.

345
00:26:36,020 --> 00:26:40,620
In the dark depths, they fought for their nation, their honor, and their lives.

346
00:26:40,621 --> 00:26:50,020
During Operation Cedar Falls, an estimated 550 Viet Cong guerrillas were either killed or captured.

347
00:26:50,480 --> 00:26:53,000
The tunnels of Vietnam will continue in a moment.

348
00:26:57,140 --> 00:27:01,100
Frustrated with their inability to quell the dogged Viet Cong resistance,

349
00:27:01,600 --> 00:27:04,800
American military leaders raised the stakes and the heat.

350
00:27:04,801 --> 00:27:11,860
From the end of 1967, massive B-52, or arc light strikes, increased in South Vietnam.

351
00:27:13,080 --> 00:27:16,220
They pounded the Iron Triangle and the surrounding danger zones.

352
00:27:18,260 --> 00:27:23,780
On the ground, Rome plows were liberally used to unearth and destroy tunnels.

353
00:27:25,440 --> 00:27:31,060
Chemical defoliants and napalm drops eliminated the jungle canopy used as cover by the enemy.

354
00:27:31,061 --> 00:27:37,780
well by the time all that was done he defoliated things all these holes started popping up all

355
00:27:37,781 --> 00:27:42,360
over the place and so there was just a lot more holes in were tunnel rats i think so they would

356
00:27:42,361 --> 00:27:46,120
just say well you know i want to go down and there's always a few of us that would

357
00:27:46,121 --> 00:27:51,100
the tunnel rats found their own reasons to justify their dangerous mission

358
00:27:51,101 --> 00:27:59,320
i enjoyed that mystique uh i enjoyed that that whisper when when the new guys would come in and

359
00:27:59,321 --> 00:28:02,500
And they'd say, that's Corey, you know, he's crazy.

360
00:28:03,100 --> 00:28:05,220
I hate to admit it, but I loved the rush.

361
00:28:05,860 --> 00:28:08,880
25th Infantryman and Tunnel Rat, Art Tejeda,

362
00:28:09,100 --> 00:28:12,480
turned the perilous task into a high-stakes treasure hunt.

363
00:28:13,760 --> 00:28:15,680
Anything in that tunnel that belongs to you,

364
00:28:15,681 --> 00:28:17,800
aside that it going to S2 Intelligence,

365
00:28:18,240 --> 00:28:20,480
like maps and paperwork and stuff like that,

366
00:28:20,520 --> 00:28:25,040
but you find uniforms, you find weapons, you know, pistols,

367
00:28:25,340 --> 00:28:27,680
both action rivals and stuff like that,

368
00:28:28,060 --> 00:28:29,240
which you can use for barter.

369
00:28:29,320 --> 00:28:34,180
which became pretty good, and it came to be a good business.

370
00:28:36,880 --> 00:28:41,480
The Viet Cong were ready as ever to repel interloping tunnel rats.

371
00:28:42,560 --> 00:28:47,780
They would tie pit vipers, and they would hang them upside down with their tails.

372
00:28:48,660 --> 00:28:52,420
So if you weren't aware of them, and you didn't look close enough,

373
00:28:53,560 --> 00:28:56,900
you'd stick your head in one of them tunnels and bang.

374
00:28:56,901 --> 00:29:04,280
I saw one unfortunate young man who was new, he didn't exactly know what he was doing, he crawled up.

375
00:29:04,700 --> 00:29:09,160
At least he knew enough to get down, but he crawled up to the tunnel and looked over the edge, and he was shot right in the face.

376
00:29:09,220 --> 00:29:10,880
The guy was just sitting right there.

377
00:29:11,960 --> 00:29:17,600
Tragic experience taught the rats to drop hand grenades into a tunnel entrance before they went in.

378
00:29:20,220 --> 00:29:26,300
The new rats adopted the now time-honored tools and techniques of the Tunnel Rat Trailblazers.

379
00:29:26,900 --> 00:29:30,620
Other soldiers would hold me by my ankles and they'd put me head first.

380
00:29:31,780 --> 00:29:36,060
I'd go in there with a .45, my right hand, flashlight on my left.

381
00:29:38,160 --> 00:29:44,520
Once in the tunnel, the new rats were prey to the same heart-pounding trials and tribulations of their predecessors.

382
00:29:46,520 --> 00:29:51,440
25th Infantryman Ken Corey volunteered to enter and search enemy tunnels.

383
00:29:51,441 --> 00:30:02,440
You were always listening to hear, you could hear a pin being pulled out of a grenade or hear a rifle being cocked.

384
00:30:04,280 --> 00:30:08,880
Infantryman Gary Schooler arrived in Vietnam in October 1967.

385
00:30:10,020 --> 00:30:12,760
Within days, he volunteered to be a tunnel rat.

386
00:30:13,860 --> 00:30:16,620
Memories of his rare duty would last a lifetime.

387
00:30:16,621 --> 00:30:20,240
There's one thing that will give me dreams and I've had dreams about,

388
00:30:20,360 --> 00:30:24,620
and that is the thought of being buried alive, of being trapped underground,

389
00:30:25,440 --> 00:30:27,260
not being able to get out, and your air slowly running out.

390
00:30:28,560 --> 00:30:30,780
And I never quite overcame that.

391
00:30:31,680 --> 00:30:35,560
I have awoken in the middle of the night in a cold sweat thinking about that.

392
00:30:36,580 --> 00:30:42,960
During an underground search, Gary Schooler and his partner got lost in a deep and dark tunnel system.

393
00:30:43,800 --> 00:30:46,020
Forget the weapons. We don't care about the glory.

394
00:30:46,021 --> 00:30:47,080
We just went out of there.

395
00:30:48,000 --> 00:30:51,220
You start thinking about, could we be lost in here for a long time?

396
00:30:52,360 --> 00:30:53,440
I remember we'd never get out.

397
00:30:54,160 --> 00:30:56,920
Finally, it seemed like we'd been down there three hours,

398
00:30:57,100 --> 00:30:58,840
finally saw a little shaft of light.

399
00:30:59,240 --> 00:31:00,740
We went to it, and it was a well.

400
00:31:01,160 --> 00:31:03,680
It popped out of the ground, and it was in the middle of a clearing.

401
00:31:03,960 --> 00:31:06,680
It was just jungle, 360 degrees all around us.

402
00:31:07,540 --> 00:31:11,420
Gary Schooler and his partner made it out spooked, but safe.

403
00:31:12,240 --> 00:31:17,560
As a rule, however, once the tunnel had been searched and all intelligence and arms recovered,

404
00:31:17,920 --> 00:31:20,780
the tunnel rats had to go back down and destroy it.

405
00:31:21,580 --> 00:31:25,680
First, they would plant the C4 explosives in the most effective spots,

406
00:31:25,960 --> 00:31:31,460
then return to the surface to coordinate the demolition with the engineers detonating the charge.

407
00:31:33,580 --> 00:31:35,560
And so we're ready, and said, yeah, okay.

408
00:31:36,440 --> 00:31:39,220
So I'd crawl back down the tunnel toward the end of the tunnel,

409
00:31:39,221 --> 00:31:41,660
and I'd pull the fuses

410
00:31:41,661 --> 00:31:43,780
and this would start the cord

411
00:31:43,781 --> 00:31:45,600
and as I'm crawling back

412
00:31:45,601 --> 00:31:47,920
I'd be pulling fuses on every satchel.

413
00:31:48,660 --> 00:31:49,680
Once I got to the opening

414
00:31:49,681 --> 00:31:52,060
they'd haul me out and we'd run like heck.

415
00:31:53,460 --> 00:31:55,980
And the ground would go up

416
00:31:55,981 --> 00:31:57,460
the ground would go down.

417
00:31:58,460 --> 00:31:59,160
End of time.

418
00:32:00,380 --> 00:32:03,500
Tragically, Art Tejeda lost his partner John Riley

419
00:32:03,501 --> 00:32:05,280
and nearly died himself

420
00:32:05,281 --> 00:32:07,300
in a terrifying tunnel search.

421
00:32:07,301 --> 00:32:10,540
flashlight pointed down the tunnel

422
00:32:10,541 --> 00:32:11,940
and there were three

423
00:32:11,941 --> 00:32:14,780
VC there looking straight

424
00:32:14,781 --> 00:32:15,481
at us

425
00:32:15,482 --> 00:32:18,680
and they opened up

426
00:32:18,681 --> 00:32:22,580
and the next thing I

427
00:32:22,581 --> 00:32:24,840
saw was this

428
00:32:24,841 --> 00:32:26,480
green, yellow, red

429
00:32:26,481 --> 00:32:28,800
color flash

430
00:32:28,801 --> 00:32:29,700
loud bang

431
00:32:29,701 --> 00:32:32,200
got my .45

432
00:32:32,201 --> 00:32:34,640
and pointed

433
00:32:34,641 --> 00:32:36,580
down the tunnel and emptied out

434
00:32:36,581 --> 00:32:43,203
the clip. I didn't hear any more, any more sounds or anything.

435
00:32:43,204 --> 00:32:45,660
I tried to get John and

436
00:32:45,661 --> 00:32:50,053
he wasn't moving. And I put my hand on his chest.

437
00:32:50,054 --> 00:32:53,280
His heart wasn't pounding or pumping

438
00:32:53,281 --> 00:32:54,600
anyway. He was dead.

439
00:32:57,340 --> 00:33:01,880
Art Tejeda was hit in the side. He was losing lots of blood and strength.

440
00:33:01,881 --> 00:33:06,620
and I could see my mother's face so lifelike

441
00:33:06,621 --> 00:33:10,600
and she told me, I said, Art, you're hurt, get out of that tunnel

442
00:33:10,601 --> 00:33:12,460
I said, no, I'm tired

443
00:33:12,461 --> 00:33:18,040
and she said, damn you, Art, get up and see the sun for one last time

444
00:33:18,041 --> 00:33:20,900
so I started crawling out

445
00:33:20,901 --> 00:33:24,360
I was just so sleepy, so tired, I felt comfortable

446
00:33:24,361 --> 00:33:26,520
I wasn't hurting

447
00:33:26,521 --> 00:33:30,200
consequently I saw a light

448
00:33:30,201 --> 00:33:33,720
and got myself out of that tunnel.

449
00:33:34,860 --> 00:33:42,980
And that was one of my scariest days in Vietnam.

450
00:33:44,260 --> 00:33:46,900
Tunnel rats John Riley and Art Tejeda

451
00:33:46,901 --> 00:33:51,120
had been victims of an ominous buildup in Viet Cong tunnel activity.

452
00:33:51,880 --> 00:33:57,740
As 1968 arrived, tunnel rats found more and more stockpiles of arms and munitions.

453
00:33:57,741 --> 00:34:03,980
It was very common to find a cache with maybe 30, 40 weapons.

454
00:34:04,420 --> 00:34:06,300
Why were all these supplies? For what?

455
00:34:06,380 --> 00:34:10,980
There's nothing out there, you know, it's an iron triangle, it's a raised desert, it's a moonscape.

456
00:34:11,140 --> 00:34:12,540
What is all this stuff doing there?

457
00:34:13,360 --> 00:34:21,260
By the end of January 1968, the real reason for the huge arms caches and underground enemy encounters would be unveiled.

458
00:34:21,261 --> 00:34:30,720
The Viet Cong were about to deliver a stunning hammer blow on American and Arvin forces in every city in South Vietnam at once.

459
00:34:31,360 --> 00:34:35,060
It would forever be remembered as the Tet Offensive.

460
00:34:37,120 --> 00:34:41,580
Some tunnels were so deep they were immune to the B-52 strikes.

461
00:34:41,840 --> 00:34:48,100
Close to half of the communist troops in the tunnel survived the attacks, while few survived on the outside.

462
00:34:48,380 --> 00:34:51,020
Modern marvels will continue in a moment.

463
00:34:51,260 --> 00:35:00,700
On January 31, 1968, the Viet Cong and the North Vietnamese Army launched the Tet Offensive.

464
00:35:06,020 --> 00:35:12,520
In one single massive blow, they hit every major city in South Vietnam at the same time.

465
00:35:13,000 --> 00:35:19,340
The communists believed the South Vietnamese Army would crumble and the people in the cities would rise up and join them.

466
00:35:19,341 --> 00:35:31,622
The formidable 9th Viet Cong Division, the guerrillas who built and fought so tenaciously from the tunnels around Cu Chi, were joined by units of the NVA and attacked

467
00:35:31,623 --> 00:35:32,323
Saigon.

468
00:35:33,940 --> 00:35:40,580
The true reason for the huge caches of arms and supplies uncovered by the tunnel rats had been revealed.

469
00:35:40,581 --> 00:35:46,720
January 31st, when the Tet Offensive hit, it all became clear, because that was a staging area.

470
00:35:47,020 --> 00:35:50,700
The troops moved in there in something like two or three days, and through the tunnels,

471
00:35:50,880 --> 00:35:54,740
they picked up their weapons, all their gear, and all this ammunition stuff had been stockpiled.

472
00:35:59,980 --> 00:36:07,300
The undefeated 9th Viet Cong Division and units of the North Vietnamese Army sprang from the tunnels and attacked Saigon.

473
00:36:08,100 --> 00:36:11,500
They were led by Vietnamese war hero, Tran Bac Deng.

474
00:36:13,620 --> 00:36:16,580
The tunnels served as the staging area.

475
00:36:16,800 --> 00:36:19,160
We launched the Tet Offensive from there.

476
00:36:20,020 --> 00:36:24,000
America's 1st and 25th Divisions and the South Vietnamese Army,

477
00:36:24,001 --> 00:36:28,740
after years of frustrating guerrilla warfare, swiftly engaged the enemy.

478
00:36:29,240 --> 00:36:33,160
The morale in most American combat units actually went up.

479
00:36:33,780 --> 00:36:34,980
Here you've got an objective.

480
00:36:35,500 --> 00:36:36,840
The enemy is there.

481
00:36:37,300 --> 00:36:39,200
They're out there. They're fighting.

482
00:36:41,400 --> 00:36:45,820
26 days later, the attack on Saigon and South Vietnam was repelled.

483
00:36:46,980 --> 00:36:49,380
Thousands of Viet Cong and NVA were killed,

484
00:36:50,100 --> 00:36:53,040
and the 9th Viet Cong Division was completely decimated.

485
00:36:54,020 --> 00:36:56,060
Tens of thousands are thought to have died.

486
00:36:57,340 --> 00:37:01,240
The Tet Offensive had failed for the Viet Cong in its military objectives.

487
00:37:02,180 --> 00:37:06,640
In the wake of Tet, American military leaders redoubled their attacks.

488
00:37:06,641 --> 00:37:10,400
B-52 bomb strikes aimed directly on the Iron Triangle

489
00:37:10,401 --> 00:37:14,080
and the underground fortifications increased in ferocity.

490
00:37:15,120 --> 00:37:19,020
The first thing you would see is these flashes, but no sound.

491
00:37:19,800 --> 00:37:21,840
And then you'd feel the rumble of the ground.

492
00:37:22,980 --> 00:37:24,380
The ground starts running like an earthquake.

493
00:37:24,780 --> 00:37:27,800
Then the next thing, the last thing, is the sound finally gets to you.

494
00:37:30,740 --> 00:37:33,800
In my opinion, the sound of the bombs falling

495
00:37:33,801 --> 00:37:37,520
was more frightening than the sound of the explosion.

496
00:37:42,180 --> 00:37:47,020
The bombs exploding two kilometers away were the most dangerous.

497
00:37:47,580 --> 00:37:50,900
The air pressure and where it caused was so strong

498
00:37:50,901 --> 00:37:53,840
would bury people trying to escape from the tunnel.

499
00:37:53,841 --> 00:38:04,420
I fought many American soldiers

500
00:38:04,421 --> 00:38:05,860
not only in Kru Chi

501
00:38:05,861 --> 00:38:07,560
but in many places

502
00:38:07,561 --> 00:38:09,040
face to face

503
00:38:09,041 --> 00:38:11,340
I'm not sure how I survived

504
00:38:11,341 --> 00:38:13,860
perhaps it was the will of God

505
00:38:13,861 --> 00:38:19,020
The few Viet Cong fighters

506
00:38:19,021 --> 00:38:20,600
that survived the Tet Offensive

507
00:38:20,601 --> 00:38:22,020
and the B-52 strikes

508
00:38:22,021 --> 00:38:23,740
soon abandoned the tunnel

509
00:38:23,741 --> 00:38:31,980
Instead of 20 men, 20 men cells protecting a certain part of the tunnel complex, they were down to two or three men.

510
00:38:32,920 --> 00:38:36,117
And that was kind of the beginning of the infront of the tunnels.

511
00:38:36,118 --> 00:38:38,380
There weren't enough people to fix the damage.

512
00:38:39,760 --> 00:38:46,460
The Viet Cong began to pull back from the Iron Triangle in Hobo Woods toward Cambodia and the north.

513
00:38:47,080 --> 00:38:50,940
Some gorilla cells retreated into caves secreted in the Black Lady Mountain.

514
00:38:50,941 --> 00:38:55,460
Despite the best efforts of the rats

515
00:38:55,461 --> 00:38:58,100
sent to destroy the Viet Cong Mountain fortification

516
00:38:58,101 --> 00:39:01,440
the guerrillas and NVA fighters held the mountain

517
00:39:01,441 --> 00:39:03,020
for the duration of the war

518
00:39:03,021 --> 00:39:06,500
We had the top, we had the bottom, we had the middle

519
00:39:06,501 --> 00:39:09,540
but it was impenetrable, it was one of those impenetrable

520
00:39:09,541 --> 00:39:12,620
fortresses and we never could drive them out of there

521
00:39:12,621 --> 00:39:13,720
it was a safe haven for them

522
00:39:13,721 --> 00:39:19,360
In 1968, 14,000 American troops died in South Vietnam

523
00:39:19,361 --> 00:39:22,720
It was the worst year for casualties on all sides.

524
00:39:23,840 --> 00:39:26,040
While accurate statistics are not available,

525
00:39:26,600 --> 00:39:31,460
some experts estimate dozens of infantrymen lost their lives on tunnel rat missions.

526
00:39:32,240 --> 00:39:36,540
For the dutiful line soldiers and courageous tunnel rats who survived,

527
00:39:36,960 --> 00:39:38,780
the flight home was a victory.

528
00:39:39,900 --> 00:39:41,400
Oh, going home.

529
00:39:42,160 --> 00:39:42,900
The Freedom Bird.

530
00:39:43,580 --> 00:39:46,620
That's what we used to call that last flight out of our country.

531
00:39:46,621 --> 00:39:56,340
And you can imagine, you're out here with a rifle, you're sloshing in a stupid rice paddy, you got all this stench, you got the bugs, and here you're looking at a

532
00:39:56,341 --> 00:39:59,280
plane and you say, gee, I hope it's my turn next.

533
00:39:59,281 --> 00:40:08,920
Today, statues of revolutionary leader Ho Chi Minh stand in the streets of Saigon.

534
00:40:09,740 --> 00:40:16,760
The bustling former capital of South Vietnam is now District 1 of the newly named Ho Chi Minh City.

535
00:40:18,640 --> 00:40:21,980
Only 40 miles north lay the tunnels of Koo Chi.

536
00:40:23,160 --> 00:40:29,200
They are now open to the public as a war memorial administered by veteran Viet Congorillas.

537
00:40:29,280 --> 00:40:36,380
As American soldiers searched above, the Viet Cong hit below.

538
00:40:36,820 --> 00:40:38,620
We have tunnels right here.

539
00:40:39,940 --> 00:40:45,920
The tunnels of Cu Chi, the underground fortification that was at the heart of the Viet Cong victory,

540
00:40:46,440 --> 00:40:52,220
is celebrated as a national monument, as significant as Boston is to the American Revolution.

541
00:40:52,221 --> 00:41:01,160
Coo Chi served not only as a battlefront, but a symbol of self-determination.

542
00:41:01,980 --> 00:41:05,740
It gave the Vietnamese people courage to fight the American soldiers.

543
00:41:09,700 --> 00:41:17,060
Colonel Chow Lam was an artillery officer with the 9th Viet Cong Division and fought from these underground bunkers.

544
00:41:22,220 --> 00:41:23,970
This is a fighting post.

545
00:41:23,971 --> 00:41:30,020
In the over 250 kilometers of tunnel, there were thousands of these fighting posts.

546
00:41:36,560 --> 00:41:42,120
Adventurous visitors are free to explore the Viet Cong's once-secret underground bunkers and tunnels.

547
00:41:42,121 --> 00:41:53,160
This is a trap door. Even if they found it, it was armed with a booby trap.

548
00:41:53,480 --> 00:41:56,820
If American soldiers tried to open it, it would explode.

549
00:41:57,100 --> 00:41:59,180
It could only be opened from the inside.

550
00:41:59,500 --> 00:42:03,420
Anyone who tries to open it from the outside would be killed in the explosion.

551
00:42:04,680 --> 00:42:08,440
Also on display, a grisly array of simple booby traps.

552
00:42:08,441 --> 00:42:11,580
cleverly crafted and cunningly placed

553
00:42:11,581 --> 00:42:14,380
these contraptions were designed to maim

554
00:42:14,381 --> 00:42:18,520
the horrifically wounded troopers would require help

555
00:42:18,521 --> 00:42:19,900
medics and helicopters

556
00:42:19,901 --> 00:42:24,640
a whole company could be tied up and be vulnerable to attack

557
00:42:24,641 --> 00:42:27,780
the stunning simplicity of these booby traps

558
00:42:27,781 --> 00:42:31,160
is a grim reminder of the brutality of war

559
00:42:31,161 --> 00:42:34,940
this is where we brought injured soldiers

560
00:42:34,941 --> 00:42:37,600
after they had been administered surgery

561
00:42:37,601 --> 00:42:45,440
The Kuti fighters were treated here, and our doctors and nurses saved many lives in a very difficult situation.

562
00:42:47,880 --> 00:42:57,680
In the nightmare that was the Vietnam War, the troops in the combat zone distinguished themselves with their commitment to duty, honor, and country.

563
00:42:58,620 --> 00:43:04,240
The tunnel rats of America's fighting forces, including the 1st and 25th Divisions,

564
00:43:04,241 --> 00:43:10,160
and Australia's three-field troop, showed uncommon valor and nerves of steel

565
00:43:10,161 --> 00:43:14,980
as they dared to chase the enemy down into the dreadful dark passageways.

566
00:43:15,620 --> 00:43:21,680
They recovered intelligence and arms that stemmed the tide of enemy insurgents and helped save lives.

567
00:43:22,200 --> 00:43:27,460
In the black, petrifying passageways, the tunnel rats disarmed lethal booby traps,

568
00:43:27,680 --> 00:43:29,880
and met the enemy face to face.

569
00:43:30,320 --> 00:43:33,460
The lucky and quick lived to tell the tale.

570
00:43:34,100 --> 00:43:36,560
It's the idea that you can overcome that fear.

571
00:43:37,060 --> 00:43:40,080
There's nothing like going through that kind of thing and winning.

572
00:43:41,360 --> 00:43:43,620
Bad things can happen to you, but if you come out ahead,

573
00:43:44,520 --> 00:43:45,940
you've got something to be proud of, I think.

574
00:43:46,900 --> 00:43:49,340
I really enjoyed the minute I got out.

575
00:43:49,740 --> 00:43:54,400
That was the best part of the mission, was getting out of that hole.

576
00:43:54,401 --> 00:44:00,360
I didn't consider myself a hero or anything like that.

577
00:44:00,500 --> 00:44:03,060
You know, I was just a grunt like the rest of them.

578
00:44:03,160 --> 00:44:07,380
You know, I've always, you know, the guys that didn't make it and stuff, you know, they were the heroes.

579
00:44:08,180 --> 00:44:10,880
We were just G.I. Joes, you know, doing our job.

580
00:44:14,720 --> 00:44:22,120
The History Channel proudly offers the program you're watching on home video for only $24.95 plus shipping and handling.

581
00:44:22,121 --> 00:44:29,000
To order, call 1-800-708-1776 or shop online at HistoryChannel.com.

582
00:44:30,280 --> 00:44:33,840
Our president was gone. Generals were in rebellion.

583
00:44:34,300 --> 00:44:37,160
In 30 days, we would cease to exist as a nation.

584
00:44:37,520 --> 00:44:41,600
But a handshake and one man's word changed history forever.

585
00:44:42,280 --> 00:44:44,420
It wasn't a month. It was a miracle.

586
00:44:44,860 --> 00:44:49,580
April 1865, April 14th at 9, only on the History Channel.

